# IEEE 754 nextDown: the next number of the format below a, on every input (milestone2.md,
# "next_up and next_down on every input"). A NaN gives a NaN; -inf gives -inf; the most negative
# finite number gives -inf; a positive number the previous one toward zero, a negative one the
# previous one away from zero; either zero the most negative subnormal number; +inf the largest
# finite number. Computed on the bit pattern, without a floating-point operation.
kernel:        next_down
input:         a
output:        r
value:         a
ensures:       neighbor r down
min_precision: 1
source:        IEEE 754 nextDown on the bit pattern (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
theorem:       proofs/theories/EntryKernels.v next_down_correct
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeEntry.v next_down_ieee_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   r = pred(a), and pred(+inf) is finite; the neighbor claim states the output for every input, infinities and NaN included

r = pred(a)
